      "geology": {
        "unit": {
          "name": "Jezero fan 2 unit",
          "symbol": "NHjf2",
          "Unit_Description": "Lightly cratered, intermediate-toned, stratified unit with margins defined by sharp scarps, located at the mouth of Neretva Vallis on the western portion of Jezero crater's floor. Occurs at higher elevation than unit NHjf1 (18.58\u00b0N, 77.48\u00b0E). Contain rugged arcuate ridges and troughs that branch out from nodes and define several overlapping lobes (Figure 7). Meter-scale sequences of alternating light and dark strata as well as some curvilinear strata (18.48\u00b0N, 77.39\u00b0E; 18.47\u00b0N, 77.37\u00b0E). Forms isolated outcrops that are <50 m in height on the Jezero crater floor east of the main unit. Some outcrops exhibit parallel stratification on their slopes (18.43\u00b0N, 77.36\u00b0E), while others do not exhibit obvious visible stratification at map scale (for example, 18.47\u00b0N, 77.50\u00b0E). Type areas: lat 18.49\u00b0N, long 77.36\u00b0E and lat 18.47\u00b0N, long 77.50\u00b0E.",
          "Unit_Interpretation": "Deltaic fan deposits emplaced during the Late Noachian to Early Hesperian, postdating and overlying portions of unit NHjf1. Presence of inlet channels (Neretva, Sava Valles) and an outlet channel (Pliva Vallis) suggests deposition into a lacustrine environment. Rugged arcuate ridges are inverted channels based on their association with the fan deposit and radiation out from various  nodes. Overlapping lobes represent branching channel deposits and distinct episodes of sediment deposition (Figure 7). Isolated outcrops could be remnants of the main delta deposit, much of which has been eroded since initial deposition."
        },
        "source": "USGS SIM 3464 contextual feature service",
        "scope": "Regional 1:75000 interpretation; authoritative GIS cross-check pending."
      },
